  "summary": "Graph pangenomes now span metagenomes, 1060 yeast isolates and gut bacteria, linking structural variation and gene-content fluidity to function and host…",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is pangenome-scale comparative inference of structural and gene-content variation. PANIMaSTA, a progressive ANI and maximum-spanning-tree workflow, targets minimally fragmented metagenomic variation graphs. A graph pangenome of 1060 Brettanomyces bruxellensis isolates detects 5972 SVs, with allopolyploids carrying more SVs and hybridization-associated SVs strongly affecting function. Gut bacterial isolate genomes show that industrialized-host strains have expanded proteomes and fluid pangenomes. PrinTE, a forward-time whole-genome simulator, infers transposon insertion and deletion rates with under 6% error and addresses genome expansion and contraction. The items do not test a shared mechanism; PrinTE is a simulation tool rather than a pangenome method, so any link to graph-based SV detection is complementary, not demonstrated.",
